ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE SPECIAL ACT ON SUPPORT FOR CENTRAL INLAND LINKED DEVELOPMENT REGIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Special Act on Support for Central Inland Linked Development Regions and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Scope of Central Inland Linked Development Regions)
"Regions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 2, subparagraph 1 of the Special Act on Support for Central Inland Linked Development Regions (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means the following regions:
1. Dong-gu, Yuseong-gu, and Daedeok-gu in Daejeon Metropolitan City;
2. Sejong Special Self-Governing City;
3. Icheon-si, Anseong-si, and Yeoju-si in Gyeonggi-do;
4. Wonju-si and Yeongwol-gun in Gangwon Special Self-Governing Province;
5. Cheongju-si, Chungju-si, Jecheon-si, Boeun-gun, Okcheon-gun, Yeongdong-gun, Jeungpyeong-gun, Jincheon-gun, Goesan-gun, Eumseong-gun, and Danyang-gun in Chungcheongbuk-do;
6. Cheonan-si and Geumsan-gun in Chungcheongnam-do;
7. Muju-gun in Jeonbuk Special Self-Governing Province;
8. Gimcheon-si, Yeongju-si, Sangju-si, Mungyeong-si, and Yecheon-gun in Gyeongsangbuk-do.

Article 3 (Formulation of Comprehensive Development Plans)
(1) A draft development plan for a central inland linked development region under the jurisdiction of a Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, or Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as a "Mayor/Do Governor"), prepared by that Mayor/Do Governor pursuant to Article 6 (4) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "draft Si/Do development plan"), shall include the following matters:
1. Matters specified in the subparagraphs of Article 6 (2) of the Act that pertain to the central inland linked development region under the jurisdiction of the relevant Mayor/Do Governor;
2. An outline of the projects to be implemented in relation to the matters under subparagraph 1 (including project scale, implementation period, and project effects) and an investment plan;
3. Other matters for reference concerning the draft Si/Do development plan.
(2) Where the head of a relevant central administrative agency is requested by the Minister of the Interior and Safety to engage in consultation on a draft comprehensive development plan for central inland linked development regions (hereinafter the plan is referred to as a "comprehensive development plan" and its draft as a "draft comprehensive development plan") pursuant to Article 6 (6) of the Act, the head shall notify the Minister of the agency's opinions within 20 days from the date of receipt of the request, unless there are special circumstances.
(3) Notwithstanding paragraph (2), where the head of a relevant central administrative agency has difficulty responding within the consultation period for unavoidable reasons, the head shall notify the Minister of the Interior and Safety of those reasons and the deadline for responding, and provide the agency's opinions by that deadline.
(4) A draft comprehensive development plan subject to deliberation by the Local Era Committee under Article 62 of the Special Act on Local Autonomy and Decentralization and Balanced Regional Development pursuant to Article 6 (6) of the Act shall be prepared taking into consideration the following matters:
1. The organic interrelationships among projects;
2. Priorities and implementation sequence for individual projects;
3. The contents of consultations with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies.

Article 4 (Holding Public Hearings)
(1) When holding a public hearing pursuant to Article 6 (5) of the Act, a Mayor/Do Governor shall announce the following matters at least once in the official bulletin and on the website of the Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Do, or Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as a "Si/Do") at least 14 days before the scheduled date of the hearing:
1. The purpose of the public hearing;
2. The scheduled date, time, and place of the public hearing;
3. An outline and the main contents of the draft Si/Do development plan;
4. Other necessary matters.
(2) A Mayor/Do Governor may hold a public hearing under paragraph (1) for the relevant Si/Do individually or jointly with other Sis/Dos.
(3) Where it is necessary to hear the opinions of residents, etc. of a Si/Gun/Gu (a Gu means an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply) concerning major projects included in a draft Si/Do development plan, a Mayor/Do Governor may hold additional public hearings in the relevant Si/Gun/Gu.
(4) When the Minister of the Interior and Safety holds a public hearing on matters not included in a draft Si/Do development plan pursuant to Article 6 (8) of the Act, the Minister shall announce the following matters at least once in the official bulletin and on the website of the Si/Do related to those matters at least 14 days before the scheduled date of the hearing:
1. The purpose of the public hearing;
2. The scheduled date, time, and place of the public hearing;
3. An outline and the main contents of the matters to be added to the draft Si/Do development plan;
4. Other necessary matters.

Article 5 (Changes to Minor Matters in Comprehensive Development Plans)
"Minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso to Article 6 (9) of the Act means any of the following:
1. Where the area or project cost of an individual project reflected in a comprehensive development plan is changed within a range of 10 percent;
2. Where the implementation period of an individual project reflected in a comprehensive development plan is shortened or extended by up to three years;
3. Where, after a major infrastructure project plan finalized under another Act has been reflected in a comprehensive development plan, that project plan is amended or abolished and the amendment or abolition is reflected in the comprehensive development plan;
4. Where the details of an individual project reflected in a comprehensive development plan are changed to an extent that does not alter the basic direction of the project.

Article 6 (Preliminary Surveys for Formulation of Comprehensive Development Plans)
(1) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 7 (1) of the Act means the following matters:
1. The current status of formulation of metropolitan plans under Article 11 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act, urban/Gun master plans under Article 18 of that Act, and plans under other statutes or regulations;
2. Other matters necessary for the formulation or amendment of a comprehensive development plan.
(2) Where a survey or measurement is to be conducted pursuant to Article 7 (1) of the Act and data from surveys or measurements conducted under other statutes or regulations exist regarding the matters to be surveyed or measured, such data may be used.

Article 7 (Annual Project Plans)
(1) A Mayor/Do Governor shall, pursuant to Article 10 (1) of the Act, formulate a draft (hereinafter referred to as a "draft annual project plan") of the annual project plan under paragraph (4) of that Article (hereinafter referred to as an "annual project plan") for the relevant year and submit it to the Minister of the Interior and Safety by January 31 each year. In such cases, explanatory materials or supporting materials concerning the projects included in the draft annual project plan shall be attached.
(2) A draft annual project plan shall include the following matters:
1. Priorities of projects;
2. Basic objectives and outlines of projects;
3. Progress in implementing projects in the preceding year and an analysis of the results;
4. Financing plans for each project;
5. Details of project costs;
6. Project implementation periods;
7. Effects of projects;
8. The financial status of the relevant Sis/Guns/Gus having jurisdiction over a central inland linked development region.
(3) If any matter in the contents of a draft annual project plan submitted by a Mayor/Do Governor pursuant to paragraph (1) requires adjustment, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may adjust such matter in consultation with the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies and the relevant Mayor/Do Governor.
(4) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall finalize draft annual project plans by March 31 each year pursuant to Article 10 (3) of the Act.
(5) Upon receiving notification, pursuant to Article 10 (4) of the Act, of an annual project plan finalized pursuant to paragraph (3) of that Article, a Mayor/Do Governor shall inform the heads of the relevant Sis/Guns/Gus (heads of Gus refer to the heads of autonomous Gus; hereinafter the same shall apply) without delay.
(6) Mayors/Do Governors and the heads of Sis/Guns/Gus shall keep annual project plans at their respective offices or publish them in the official gazettes and on the websites of the relevant Sis/Dos and Sis/Guns/Gus so that residents may inspect them for at least 14 days.
(7) "Minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso to Article 10 (5) of the Act means any of the following:
1. Changing the area or project cost of an individual project reflected in an annual project plan by up to 10/100;
2. Reflecting the details of the amendment or abolition of a major infrastructure project plan in an annual project plan, where such major infrastructure project plan has been amended or abolished after being finalized under another Act and reflected in the annual project plan;
3. Changing the details of an individual project reflected in an annual project plan to the extent that its basic direction remains unchanged.

Article 8 (Establishment and Operation of Support Departments, etc.)
(1) The Minister of the Interior and Safety may establish a support department pursuant to Article 11 (3) of the Act to efficiently perform duties concerning the development of central inland linked development regions.
(2) Where necessary for the operation of the support department, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may request a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu to second staff under his or her authority. In such cases, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may adjust the scale, duration, methods, etc. of staffing support following consultation with the relevant Mayor/Do Governor or head of a Si/Gun/Gu, taking into account the comprehensive development plan, etc.
(3) A Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu who has been requested by the Minister of the Interior and Safety to second staff under his or her authority pursuant to paragraph (2) shall take the measures necessary for the secondment unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
(4) Matters necessary for the operation of the support department, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be determined by the Minister of the Interior and Safety.

Article 9 (Composition of the Council for Central Inland Linked Development Regions)
(1) The Council for Central Inland Linked Development Regions under Article 12 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Council") shall consist of not more than 32 members, including 8 co-chairpersons.
(2) The co-chairpersons of the Council shall be the Mayor of Daejeon Metropolitan City, the Mayor of Sejong Special Self-Governing City, the Governor of Gyeonggi-do, the Governor of Gangwon Special Self-Governing Province, the Governor of Chungcheongbuk-do, the Governor of Chungcheongnam-do, the Governor of Jeonbuk Special Self-Governing Province, and the Governor of Gyeongsangbuk-do, and the members of the Council other than the co-chairpersons shall be the following persons:
1. The chairpersons of the Si/Do councils of central inland linked development regions;
2. The heads of local government-funded research institutes established by Mayors/Do Governors pursuant to the Act on the Establishment and Operation of Local Government-Funded Research Institutes;
3. Eight persons, one appointed by each co-chairperson, from among persons with extensive knowledge and experience in fields related to the development of central inland linked development regions, including regional development, national land planning, tourism, logistics, science and technology, administration and finance, and the environment.
(3) The term of office of an appointed member under paragraph (2) 3 shall be 2 years, and such member may be reappointed for only one consecutive term.
(4) A co-chairperson may dismiss an appointed member under paragraph (2) 3 from office if the member falls under any of the following:
1. Where the member becomes unable to perform his or her duties for an extended period due to physical or mental incapacity, etc.;
2. Where the member has committed misconduct in connection with his or her duties;
3. Where the member is deemed unsuitable to serve as a member due to neglect of duties, impairment of dignity, or other reasons;
4. Where the member expresses his or her own view that it is difficult to perform his or her duties.

Article 10 (Operation of the Council)
(1) Each co-chairperson shall represent the Council and exercise overall control over its affairs.
(2) Meetings of the Council shall be convened by the co-chairpersons in consultation with one another, and the Mayor/Do Governor determined through consultation among the co-chairpersons shall preside over the meetings.
(3) A meeting of the Council shall be convened with the attendance of a majority of all incumbent members, and resolutions shall be adopted with the affirmative votes of a majority of the members present.
(4) Where a co-chairperson deems it necessary for consultation, such co-chairperson may have the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, relevant public officials, or interested parties attend and hear their opinions.
(5) Matters necessary for the operation of the Council, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be determined by the co-chairpersons following a resolution of the Council.

Article 11 (Allowances)
Allowances and travel expenses may be paid, within budgetary limits, to members attending meetings of the Council: Provided, That this shall not apply where a member who is a public official attends in direct connection with the member's official duties.

Article 12 (Project Implementers)
"A person meeting the qualifications prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as capital requirements" in subparagraph 5 of Article 13 of the Act means any of the following persons:

1. A person registered in a category of business that performs general construction works under subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Framework Act on the Construction Industry (limited to civil engineering construction business and civil engineering and building construction business), whose assessed construction capacity for the relevant year publicly announced pursuant to Article 23 of that Act or capital is at least equal to the relevant project costs specified in the annual project plan (excluding compensation costs);

2. A person who owns land constituting at least 50 percent of the area of land subject to the project included in the annual project plan;

3. A real estate trust company entrusted with land constituting at least one-third of the area of land subject to the project included in the annual project plan.

Article 13 (Approval for Implementation of Projects)
(1) A person intending to obtain approval for implementation of a project pursuant to Article 14 (1) of the Act shall, pursuant to paragraph (3) of that Article, submit an application for approval of project implementation in attached Form 1 to the authority granting approval for the project under paragraph (1) of that Article (hereinafter referred to as the "project approval authority"), accompanied by a project plan containing the following matters:

1. An outline of the project (including the name, necessity, details, and scale of the project, and the project implementation period);

2. A location map and relevant drawings of the project implementation area;

3. The amount of project costs and methods of financing;

4. Relevant documents under Article 15 (2) of the Act;

5. Plans for purchase, compensation, etc. concerning land, etc. located in the project implementation area and measures for relocation of residents;

6. Plans for relocation and demolition of public facilities and plans for installation of replacement facilities;

7. In the case of a project subject to environmental impact assessment under Article 22 of the Environmental Impact Assessment Act or a project subject to small-scale environmental impact assessment under Article 43 of that Act, documents concerning the details of consultation on the environmental impact assessment or small-scale environmental impact assessment under Article 29 or 45 of that Act.

(2) A person intending to obtain approval of changes to a project pursuant to the main clause of Article 14 (2) of the Act shall submit to the project approval authority an application for approval of project changes in attached Form 2, accompanied by a revised plan containing the matters to be changed among those specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1).

(3) "Minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso of Article 14 (2) of the Act means any of the following:

1. A change within 10 percent of the project area or project costs;

2. A change within 10 percent of the area for installation of facilities or equipment.

(4) When deciding whether to grant approval pursuant to Article 14 (4) of the Act, the project approval authority shall take the following matters into consideration:

1. Investment capacity:

(a) Validity of the project implementation plan and investment plan;

(b) Status of land ownership in the area subject to the project and the possibility of securing development and use rights;

2. Securing investment funds:

(a) The proportion of equity capital in total investment costs and the appropriateness of the financing plan;

(b) The appropriateness of the ratio of equity capital to externally supplied capital and of the debt ratio;

3. Measures to prevent environmental pollution:

(a) Appropriateness of measures to conserve the natural environment in the project implementation area and neighboring areas;

(b) Appropriateness of restoration and rehabilitation of the natural environment damaged by project implementation;

4. Other matters:

(a) Matters concerning the return of development gains to the local community;

(b) Contribution to employment of local residents and promotion of related industries.

(5) Upon receiving an application for approval or approval of changes under paragraph (1) or (2), the project approval authority shall process the application within 20 days from the date of its receipt: Provided, That the period may be extended by up to 10 days where unavoidable reasons exist.

(6) Where the project approval authority extends the period for processing an application for approval or approval of changes pursuant to the proviso of paragraph (5), it shall notify the applicant of that fact.

(7) Where the project approval authority grants approval for implementation of a project pursuant to Article 14 (4) of the Act, it shall, pursuant to paragraph (5) of that Article, publicly notify a project implementation plan containing the following matters in the official gazette and publicly announce it on its website:

1. Name of the project;

2. Purpose and outline of the project;

3. Location and area of the project implementation area;

4. Name (in the case of a corporation, meaning the name of the corporation and the name of its representative) and address (in the case of a corporation, meaning the location of its principal office) of the project implementer under the portion of Article 13 of the Act other than its subparagraphs (hereinafter referred to as the "project implementer");

5. Project implementation period;

6. A detailed list of land, etc. located in the project implementation area, including its location, lot number, land category, and area.

(8) Where the project approval authority grants approval of changes to a project pursuant to Article 14 (4) of the Act, it shall, pursuant to paragraph (5) of that Article, publicly notify the approved changes in the official gazette and publicly announce them on the relevant website.

(9) Where a project implementer referred to in subparagraphs 1 and 2 of Article 13 of the Act has formulated a project implementation plan in consultation with the project approval authority pursuant to Article 14 (8) of the Act, it shall publicly notify a project implementation plan containing the matters specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (7) in the official gazette issued by that project approval authority and publicly announce it on the website of the project approval authority.

Article 14 (Completion Inspections)
(1) A project implementer (excluding the State and local governments) seeking a completion inspection pursuant to Article 16 (1) of the Act shall submit to the project approval authority a construction completion report in attached Form 3, accompanied by the following documents and drawings:

1. A completion statement (including as-built design documents and completion photographs);

2. A map of cadastral survey results;

3. A statement of land areas by use and owner;

4. A statement and drawings concerning the vesting of public facilities, etc.;

5. A comparative map of the former and new cadastral boundaries.

(2) The completion inspection certificate under Article 16 (3) of the Act shall be in attached Form 4.

(3) Pursuant to Article 16 (3) of the Act, the project approval authority shall publicly announce completion of construction in the official gazette and on the website of the local government having jurisdiction, including the following matters:

1. Name of the project;

2. Project implementer;

3. Location of the project implementation site;

4. Area of the project implementation site and the area by use;

5. Date of completion;

6. Matters concerning disposition of major facilities.

Article 15 (Designation of Central Inland Linked Development Districts)
(1) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety intends to designate a central inland linked development district pursuant to Article 17 (1) of the Act, the Minister shall consider the following matters:

1. Whether the area is capable of creating drivers of economic growth for a central inland linked development region and is expected to have substantial spillover effects on neighboring areas;

2. Whether the area is capable of efficiently utilizing the human and material resources of a central inland linked development region;

3. Whether the area has infrastructure, such as railways, roads, and ports, that enables it to form a transportation and logistics hub;

4. Whether the area needs to be developed into a hub city for a regional living area.

(2) Public notices under Article 17 (2) and (4) of the Act shall each be given by publication in the Official Gazette.

Article 16 (National Subsidy Rate)
The subsidy rate under the latter part of Article 18 (1) of the Act (hereinafter in this Article referred to as the "support subsidy rate") shall be the rate obtained by adding 20 percentage points to the base subsidy rate under Article 9 (1) 2 of the Subsidy Management Act; where the rate obtained by adding 20 percentage points to the base subsidy rate exceeds 80 percent, the support subsidy rate shall be 80 percent: Provided, That where the base subsidy rate is at least 80 percent, that base subsidy rate shall be the support subsidy rate.

Article 17 (Support through Local Subsidies)
In addition to the subsidies under Articles 6, 9, and 9-3 of the Local Subsidy Act, where a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu submits a request on the grounds that special financial needs have arisen from demand related to pending regional issues for the implementation of central inland linked development projects, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may, pursuant to Article 18 (2) of the Act, examine the request and grant special subsidies out of local subsidies within the limits of the funds available for their operation.

Article 18 (Special Cases concerning the Mountainous Districts Management Act)
Pursuant to Article 19 (1) of the Act, the conversion or temporary use of mountainous districts shall be prohibited in conserved mountainous districts under the Mountainous Districts Management Act that are included in central inland linked development districts (excluding areas subject to restrictions on the conversion or temporary use of mountainous districts under Article 9 (1) of that Act), except where such conversion or temporary use is undertaken to engage in any of the following activities:

1. An activity falling under any of the subparagraphs of Article 12 (1) or any of the subparagraphs of Article 12 (2) of the Mountainous Districts Management Act;

2. Installation of facilities necessary for projects included in an annual project plan.

Article 19 (Identification Documents for Public Officials Conducting Entry and Inspection)
The identification document indicating the authority of a public official conducting entry and inspection under Article 21 (2) of the Act shall be in attached Form 5.

Article 20 (Public Notice of Revocation of Approval, etc.)
Where a project approval authority has imposed a disposition under Article 22 (1) of the Act, the authority shall give public notice of the following matters in the official gazette and on the website of the competent local government:

1. Name of the project;

2. Project implementer;

3. Location of the project site;

4. Details of and reasons for the disposition;

5. Other matters that the project approval authority deems necessary to give public notice of.

